  "question": {
    "text": "Shown in the figure are two point charges <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>+</mo><mi>Q</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><mi>Q</mi></math> inside the cavity of a spherical shell. The charges are kept near the surface of the cavity on opposite sides of the centre of the shell. If <math><msub><mrow><mi>&#963;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math> is the surface charge on the inner surface and <math><msub><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math> net charge on it and <math><msub><mrow><mi>&#963;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></math> the surface charge on the outer surface and <math><msub><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></math> net charge on it then:<br /><br /><img src=\"assets\\qt0239_14dd760d3950.png\" style=\"width: 170px; height: 168px;\" />"
  },
