  "solution": {
    "text": "By the property of electrostatic shielding in the conductors <math><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math> in the conductor. So, electric flux,<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math> through a dotted Gaussian surface as shown<br /><br /><img src=\"assets\\st0239_16570d1dfd67.png\" style=\"width: 170px; height: 172px;\" /><br /><br />The net enclosed charge through the Gaussian surface<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>,<math><mo>&#8658;</mo></math>&nbsp;The net charge <math><msub><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math> on the inner surface<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>, but the equal and opposite induced charge on the surface will be distributed non uniformly on the inner surface<br /><br />So, <math><msub><mrow><mi>&#963;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math><br /><br /><math><mo>&#8757;</mo><msub><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math> on the inner surface<br /><br />So, net charge <math><msub><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math> on the outer surface as conductor is neutral but <math><mo>&#8757;</mo></math> outer surface is free from any electric field so no charge density exists on the outer surface. So, <math><msub><mrow><mi>&#963;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math> ."
